      "message": "It looks like aosp/4368094 already covers this. `__acct_update_integrals()` converts each sample to kernel pages with `mm_kernel_pages()`, using the mm the task has at that moment, so a later exec doesn\u0027t change the unit of `acct_rss_mem1` and `acct_vm_mem1`. `xacct_add_tsk()` then converts them with the kernel `PAGE_SIZE`, since `kernel/tsacct.c` doesn\u0027t opt into `\u003clinux/p3s_user_pages.h\u003e`, and it already reports the high-water marks in kB with `mm_pages_to_kb()`.\n\nAs far as I can see, the only difference for a 4KB process is that each sample is no longer rounded up to the next 16KB (12KB at most), and `taskstats_p3s` in aosp/4367986 accepts results within 25% on a 32MB workload. On the other hand, the patch changes how every architecture accumulates the integrals, and it also realigns the high-water lines and drops `MB`.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this patch? If there\u0027s a case I\u0027m missing, could the commit message say what fails without it?",
